<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		iOS / iPadOS sideload (development) entitlements.

		The previous version of this file used *macOS App Sandbox* keys
		(com.apple.security.app-sandbox, com.apple.security.files.*,
		com.apple.security.network.client, com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit, ...).
		Those keys are macOS-only: iOS ignores them AND they are absent from a
		normal iOS provisioning profile.  Signing an iOS device build with them
		produces an embedded-entitlements/provisioning-profile mismatch that
		prevents launch on a real iPad.  iOS apps are sandboxed by default, so
		no "app-sandbox" entitlement exists or is needed on iPadOS.

		get-task-allow=true is what development/sideload signing tools
		(AltStore, Sideloadly, Xcode "Run") put in the provisioning profile.
		The embedded entitlements must be a subset of that profile, which is
		why this file contains only that one key.  For App Store / ad-hoc
		distribution signing use CasioEmuMsvc.release.entitlements instead
		(or no entitlements file at all).

		JIT and unsigned executable memory need NO iOS entitlement to be
		*forbidden* — they simply are not available to third-party iPadOS apps.
		This port ships the portable interpreter and data-only plugins, so it
		does not request (and cannot request) any JIT capability.
	-->
	<key>get-task-allow</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
